tel 1.0

base vicarious
import soundness

info   values indexed by the field paths of a type, addressed through a compile-time-checked proxy
detail
    _Vicarious_ treats the structure of a case class — every field reachable from it, at any
    depth, and the whole value itself — as a fixed set of **paths**, `opponent.name` rather than
    `name`, and lets code name those paths through a **proxy**: a stand-in value of the type
    with no fields of its own, on which each selection is resolved as the code compiles into the
    index of the path it names. A path the type does not have is a compile error, so no path is
    ever written as text.

    Two structures are built on the proxy. A `Catalog[Key, Value]` holds one `Value` for every
    path of `Key`, computed by one function from a value of `Key`, read by path, transformed
    with `map` and combined entry by entry with `braid`. A `Specific` maps some paths of a type
    to typeclass instances, for a derivation (`wisteria`'s, and so `jacinta`'s, `stratiform`'s
    and the other codecs') to use at exactly those paths in place of the instance for the
    field's type.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)
    case class Duel(challenger: Person, opponent: Person)

    val duel = Duel(Person(t"Laertes", 25), Person(t"Hamlet", 30))
    val labels: Catalog[Duel, Text] = catalog(duel)([field] => (field: field) => field.toString.tt)

    labels(proxy ?=> proxy.opponent.name)    // t"Hamlet"
    labels(proxy ?=> proxy.opponent.title)   // does not compile
    ```

    Paths are flattened depth-first with the root first: for `Duel` they are the whole `Duel`,
    `challenger`, `challenger.name`, `challenger.age`, `opponent`, `opponent.name` and
    `opponent.age`, so a catalog over `Duel` has seven entries, and two catalogs over the same key
    align position for position.

    The library has a single source set, `vicarious.core`. `Catalog`, `catalog`, `Proxy`,
    `Specific`, `root` and `specifically` are exported to the `soundness` umbrella; the
    `Proxy` given is in its companion, in implicit scope, so nothing is imported by name.

refer vicarious.Catalog
refer vicarious.catalog
refer vicarious.Proxy
refer vicarious.Specific
refer vicarious.specifically

entry .catalog   builds a `Catalog` by applying one polymorphic function to a value and to every field within it
    `catalog(value)(lambda)` is a macro which walks the case-class structure of `value`'s type
    as the code compiles, and applies `lambda` — a pure polymorphic function `[field] =>
    (field: field) -> result` — to the whole value and then to each field, depth-first, in
    declaration order. The function must accept a field of any type, so it may do with one only
    what every value supports. A `ClassTag` for the result type is needed, since the entries are
    held in an array.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)
    case class Duel(challenger: Person, opponent: Person)

    val duel = Duel(Person(t"Laertes", 25), Person(t"Hamlet", 30))
    val labels: Catalog[Duel, Text] = catalog(duel)([field] => (field: field) => field.toString.tt)
    labels.size   // 7: the whole Duel, challenger, challenger.name, challenger.age,
                  //    opponent, opponent.name, opponent.age
    ```

entry .Catalog   one value for every field path of a key type, read by a path checked as the code compiles
  detail
      A `Catalog[Key, Value]` holds a `Value` at each position of `Key`'s flattened structure —
      the whole value first, then every field depth-first — in `#values`. It is built with
      `catalog`, read with `#apply` and a path written through a proxy, transformed with `#map`,
      overridden at named paths with `.brush`, and combined with another catalog over the same
      key with `#braid`. The key type is what makes two catalogs combinable and what checks
      every path used to read one.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label A catalog of the label of every field
        code
            import soundness.*

            case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)
            case class Duel(challenger: Person, opponent: Person)

            val duel = Duel(Person(t"Laertes", 25), Person(t"Hamlet", 30))
            val labels = catalog(duel)([field] => (field: field) => field.toString.tt)

            labels(proxy ?=> proxy.opponent.name)   // t"Hamlet"
      frame
        label Mapped to a catalog of widths, addressed by the same paths
        code
            import soundness.*

            case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)
            case class Duel(challenger: Person, opponent: Person)

            val duel = Duel(Person(t"Laertes", 25), Person(t"Hamlet", 30))
            val labels = catalog(duel)([field] => (field: field) => field.toString.tt)
            val widths: Catalog[Duel, Int] = labels.map(_.length)

            widths(proxy ?=> proxy.opponent.name)   // 6
      frame
        label Braided with the labels, entry by entry
        code
            import soundness.*

            case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)
            case class Duel(challenger: Person, opponent: Person)

            val duel = Duel(Person(t"Laertes", 25), Person(t"Hamlet", 30))
            val labels = catalog(duel)([field] => (field: field) => field.toString.tt)
            val widths: Catalog[Duel, Int] = labels.map(_.length)
            val cells = labels.braid(widths)((label, width) => t"$label ($width)")

            cells(proxy ?=> proxy.opponent.name)   // t"Hamlet (6)"
      ##
      ```

      This is how a table of per-field settings is assembled: catalogs of labels, widths and
      whatever else matters, built separately and braided together, each addressed by the same
      paths. A `Catalog` is `Findable`.
  refer vicarious.catalog
  refer vicarious.Proxy
  entry .brush   a copy of a catalog with the entries at some paths replaced, by a partial function of paths
      An extension on any `Catalog`, needing the derived `Proxy` for the key type. The argument
      is a context function from the proxy to a partial function over proxies: each `case` names
      a path, as a pattern on the proxy, and gives its new value; every path not matched keeps
      its entry.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)
      case class Duel(challenger: Person, opponent: Person)

      val duel = Duel(Person(t"Laertes", 25), Person(t"Hamlet", 30))
      val labels = catalog(duel)([field] => (field: field) => field.toString.tt)

      val redacted = labels.brush(proxy ?=> { case proxy.opponent.name() => t"(withheld)" })
      ```
  entry #values   the entries, one per path, the whole value first and then each field depth-first
  entry #size   the number of entries: one for the whole value and one for each field at any depth
  entry #apply   the entry at a path, written as selections on a proxy of the key type
      `catalog(proxy ?=> proxy.opponent.name)` resolves the path as the code compiles into the index
      of its entry; `proxy ?=> proxy` is the entry for the whole value, and a field the type does
      not have is a compile error. `challenger.name` and `opponent.name` are different entries,
      since a path is a position, not a name.
  entry #map   a catalog over the same key with a function applied to every entry
      The paths are unchanged, so any path valid on this catalog is valid on the result. A
      `ClassTag` for the new value type is needed.
  entry #braid   combines two catalogs over the same key, entry by entry, with a function of both values
      `left.braid(right)(lambda)` needs no matching of names: catalogs over the same key align
      position for position. A `ClassTag` for the result type is needed.
  entry #tie   evaluates a block with this catalog and its key's proxy in context, so a path reads its entry
      Inside the block, the catalog is a contextual value, so `proxy.opponent.name()` — a path
      followed by `()`, which is `Proxy#apply` — reads that path's entry directly; several paths
      may be read in one expression.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)
      case class Duel(challenger: Person, opponent: Person)

      val duel = Duel(Person(t"Laertes", 25), Person(t"Hamlet", 30))
      val labels = catalog(duel)([field] => (field: field) => field.toString.tt)

      labels.tie((catalog, proxy) ?=> t"${proxy.challenger.name()} and ${proxy.opponent.name()}")
      ```

entry .Proxy   a stand-in for a value of a key type, whose field selections are resolved to path indices as the code compiles
  detail
      A `Proxy[Key, Value, Ordinal]` stands at one path of `Key`: `Ordinal` is that path's index
      as a literal type (`0` for the root), and `#id` the same index at runtime. Selecting a
      field on a proxy (`proxy.opponent.name`) is a `selectDynamic` macro which looks the extended
      path up in `Key`'s flattened structure and gives the proxy for it, so a field the type does
      not have is a compile error, _vicarious: `opponent.title` is not a valid field path_.

      The root proxy for a key type is `.derived`, in implicit scope; `Catalog#apply` and
      `Catalog#tie` provide it, and `specifically` provides one as `root`. A proxy is used three
      ways: as an address (`Catalog#apply`), to read an entry from a catalog in context
      (`#apply`), and as an extractor in a pattern (`#unapply`), which is how `specifically` and
      `.brush` name paths in their `case`s. A `Proxy` is `Findable`.
  refer vicarious.Catalog
  refer vicarious.specifically
  entry .derived   the root proxy for a key type, refined with its fields' proxies so that selections type-check
      A `transparent inline given` macro, in implicit scope: its type is `Proxy[Key, Value, 0]`
      refined with a member for each field, recursively, each a proxy whose `Ordinal` is that
      field's path index.
  entry #selectDynamic   the proxy for a field of this proxy's path, or a compile error if there is none
      Called by a selection such as `proxy.opponent`; not written directly.
  entry #id   the index of this proxy's path in its key type's flattened structure, the root being `0`
  entry #apply   the entry at this proxy's path in the `Catalog` in context
      `proxy.opponent.name()` needs a `Catalog[Key, Value]` as a contextual value, as `Catalog#tie`
      provides.
  entry #unapply   matches a proxy for the same path, so that a path can be a `case` pattern
      `case root.opponent.name() =>` matches exactly the proxy whose `#id` is that of
      `opponent.name`.

entry .Specific   typeclass instances for named field paths of a type, for a derivation to use there
  detail
      `Key is Specific over Typeclass` records exceptions to a generic derivation: for some
      field paths of `Key`, the instance to use at that exact path rather than the one the
      field's type would resolve to. A given in scope changes every field of a type at once; a
      `Specific` changes one path and nowhere else. It is built with `specifically`, which
      checks each path and each instance's type as the code compiles, and is consulted by
      `wisteria`'s derivations, and so by the codecs derived through them.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import formatting.compactJsonFormatting

      case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)
      case class Duel(challenger: Person, opponent: Person)

      val shout: Text is Json.Encodable = Json.Encodable(() => Morphology.Str): text => Json(text.upper)

      given (Duel is Specific over Json.Encodable) =
        specifically:
          case root.opponent.name() => shout

      Duel(Person(t"Laertes", 25), Person(t"Hamlet", 30)).in[Json].show
      // t"""{"challenger":{"name":"Laertes","age":25},"opponent":{"name":"HAMLET","age":30}}"""
      ```

      A `Specific` whose `#Transport` is for a different typeclass is ignored by a derivation.
  refer vicarious.specifically
  refer vicarious.root
  entry .Cases   the partial function of field-path cases `specifically` reads: proxy patterns to instances
      `Specific.Cases[Key]` is `PartialFunction[Proxy[Key, Any, Nat], Any]`. `specifically`
      reads its `case`s from the tree as the code compiles; the function itself is never run.
  entry #Self   the key type whose field paths are overridden
  entry #Transport   the typeclass the overriding instances are for, such as `Json.Encodable`
  entry #instances   each overridden dotted path (`"opponent.name"`), with its instance, type-erased
      The derivation reads an instance from this map and re-types it at the field it visits.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      duelSpecific.instances.keys   // Set("opponent.name", "challenger.name")
      ```

entry .specifically   builds a `Specific` from `case` patterns naming field paths through `root`
    `specifically { case root.opponent.name() => instance; … }` is a macro whose `Key` and
    `Typeclass` come from the expected type (`Duel is Specific over Json.Encodable`). For each
    `case`, the proxy pattern gives the path, and the instance must be a `Typeclass` for the type
    at that path, or the definition does not compile, _vicarious: the instance for this path is
    not a …_; an unknown path is likewise a compile error.

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    ##
    val custom: Duel is Specific over (Codec in Json) =
      specifically:
        case root.opponent.name() => nameCodec
        case root.challenger.age()  => ageCodec
    ```

    A path ending at a collection takes an instance for the collection, so re-deriving it there
    under a local given re-encodes its elements in that one place:

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    ##
    given (Troupe is Specific over Json.Encodable) =
      specifically:
        case root.players() =>
          given Person is Json.Encodable = nameOnly
          summon[List[Person] is Json.Encodable]
    ```

entry .root   the root proxy inside `specifically`'s cases, from which every path is selected
    Resolves to the proxy which `specifically` provides as a contextual value tagged `"root"`, so
    `case root.opponent.name() =>` needs no binder. Outside a `specifically` block there is no such
    value, and `root` does not compile.

entry .internal   the macros behind `catalog`, `Proxy` and `specifically`
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: `catalog`, `Proxy.derived`, `Proxy#selectDynamic` and `specifically`
      splice these. The path helpers flatten a case class's fields depth-first with the root,
      `""`, at index 0.
  entry .catalog   builds a `Catalog` from a value and a polymorphic function
  entry .fieldNames   the dotted paths of a product's fields, depth-first, under a prefix
  entry .paths   every flattened path of a product, with the root path `""` first
  entry .fieldReprs   the type at every flattened path of a product, aligned with `.paths`
  entry .dereference   resolves a field selection on a proxy to the proxy for the extended path
  entry .specific   builds a `Specific` from the `case`s of a partial function of paths
  entry .proxy   builds the refined root proxy for a key type
